}, {"id": "661f51_p3", "source_url": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/23351796/django-block-internet-connection-for-testing-purposes", "source_title": "python - Django: block internet connection for testing purposes - Stack Overflow", "text": "I'd like to make sure my unit tests do not try to connect to Internet, is there a way to raise an exception when they do?\nThere was a similar question [Python: block network connections for testing purposes?](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/18601828/python-block-network-connections-for-testing-purposes), but the solution proposed there blocks all socket connections, including the database ones, which is not acceptable for Django tests.\n\n- [python](/questions/tagged/python)\n- [django](/questions/tagged/django)\n- [unit-testing](/questions/tagged/unit-testing)\n- [testing](/questions/tagged/testing)\n\n[Share](/q/23351796)\n[Improve this question](/posts/23351796/edit)\n\n[edited May 23, 2017 at 11:45](/posts/23351796/revisions)\n\n[Community](/users/-1/community)Bot\n111 silver badge\n\n[Ivan Ivanov](/users/905318/ivan-ivanov)\n2,08211 gold badge1919 silver badges3030 bronze badges2\n\npossible duplicate of [How can I disable a third party API when executing Django unit tests?](http://stackoverflow.com/questions/3730286/how-can-i-disable-a-third-party-api-when-executing-django-unit-tests)", "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "corpus_id": null}, {"id": "661f51_p4", "source_url": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/23351796/django-block-internet-connection-for-testing-purposes", "source_title": "python - Django: block internet connection for testing purposes - Stack Overflow", "text": "Brad Beattie\n–\n                        [Brad Beattie](/users/183948/brad-beattie)\n2014-04-28 23:21:02 +00:00CommentedApr 28, 2014 at 23:21\n\n@BradBeattie definitely not, that question is about mocking API calls, I just want to make sure I have mocked everything that connects to the Internet and didn't miss a thing (I want the tests to fail otherwise)\n\nIvan Ivanov\n–\n                        [Ivan Ivanov](/users/905318/ivan-ivanov)\n2014-04-28 23:56:14 +00:00CommentedApr 28, 2014 at 23:56\n[\n                        Add a comment\n                    ](#) |\n\n##\n3 Answers                                        3\n\nSorted by:\n        [\n            Reset to default\n        ](/questions/23351796/django-block-internet-connection-for-testing-purposes?answertab=scoredesc#tab-top)\n\nFound out a way to do this. You can paste this into your settings.if 'test' in sys.argv:\n\n# Block Internet access during tests\n    import urllib2\n    import httplib\n    import httplib2\n\ndef _raise_http_error(*args, **kwargs):\n        raise urllib2.URLError(\"I told you not to use the Internet!\")", "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "corpus_id": null}, {"id": "661f51_p5", "source_url": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/23351796/django-block-internet-connection-for-testing-purposes", "source_title": "python - Django: block internet connection for testing purposes - Stack Overflow", "text": "class AngryHandler(urllib2.BaseHandler):\n        handler_order = 1\n\ndef default_open(self, req):\n            _raise_http_error()\n\nopener = urllib2.build_opener(AngryHandler)\n    urllib2.install_opener(opener)\n\n_HTTPHandler = urllib2.HTTPHandler\n    urllib2.HTTPHandler = AngryHandler\n\nhttplib.HTTPConnection.connect = lambda self: None\n    httplib.HTTPSConnection.connect = lambda self: None\n    httplib.HTTPConnection.request = _raise_http_error\n    httplib.HTTPSConnection.request = _raise_http_error\n    httplib2.Http.request = _raise_http_error\n\n[Share](/a/23378791)\n[Improve this answer](/posts/23378791/edit)\n\n[edited May 26, 2017 at 17:10](/posts/23378791/revisions)\n\n[Ivan Ivanov](/users/905318/ivan-ivanov)\n2,08211 gold badge1919 silver badges3030 bronze badges\n\nSign up to request clarification or add additional context in comments.\n\nmlissner\n[mlissner](/users/64911/mlissner) [Over a year ago](#comment63115735_23378791)", "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "corpus_id": null}, {"id": "661f51_p6", "source_url": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/23351796/django-block-internet-connection-for-testing-purposes", "source_title": "python - Django: block internet connection for testing purposes - Stack Overflow", "text": "Seems easier to block socket, which I think is upstream of all of these libraries. There's an example here that seems to do something like that: [stackoverflow.com/a/18601897/64911](http://stackoverflow.com/a/18601897/64911) 2016-06-15T05:00:12.257Z+00:00\n\nTake a look at vcrpy: [https://github.com/kevin1024/vcrpy](https://github.com/kevin1024/vcrpy)\nThe first time you run the test it'll make the external request. Every time after that it'll use a fixture that it stored from the first call. The external requests never get made again, which is what you're trying to accomplish.\nUsage is dead simple using a decorator on your unit test:@vcr.use_cassette('fixtures/vcr_cassettes/synopsis.yaml')\ndef test_iana():\n    response = urllib2.urlopen('http://www.iana.org/domains/reserved').read()\n    assert 'Example domains' in response\n\nSupports any request made using the following libraries:\n- urllib2\n- urllib3\n- http.client (python3)\n- requests (both 1.x and 2.x\nversions)\n- httplib2\n- boto\n- Tornado’s AsyncHTTPClient\n\n[Share](/a/34831181)\n[Improve this answer](/posts/34831181/edit)", "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "corpus_id": null}, {"id": "661f51_p7", "source_url": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/23351796/django-block-internet-connection-for-testing-purposes", "source_title": "python - Django: block internet connection for testing purposes - Stack Overflow", "text": "[MNM](/users/420678/mnm)\n2,18911 gold badge1313 silver badges1313 bronze badges\n\n[httpretty](https://github.com/gabrielfalcao/HTTPretty) solves this problem.\nWrite a custom test runner where you disable only 3rd party API calls, only in your test suite.# common/test_runner.py\n\nimport httpretty\nfrom django.test.runner import DiscoverRunner\n\nclass CustomTestRunner(DiscoverRunner):\n    def run_tests(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        with httpretty.enabled(allow_net_connect=False):\n            return super().run_tests(*args, **kwargs)\n\nadd this new test runner to your settingsTEST_RUNNER = \"common.test_runner.CustomTestRunner\"\n\nAnd from now on all external API calls have to be mocked or httpretty.errors.UnmockedError will be raised.\nAlternatively, if your project uses [responses](https://github.com/getsentry/responses) library, responses.activate or responses.start should work similarly.\n\n[Share](/a/68838903)\n[Improve this answer](/posts/68838903/edit)\n\n[edited Jan 28, 2022 at 11:49](/posts/68838903/revisions)", "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "corpus_id": null}, {"id": "661f51_p8", "source_url": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/23351796/django-block-internet-connection-for-testing-purposes", "source_title": "python - Django: block internet connection for testing purposes - Stack Overflow", "text": "[Tom Wojcik](/users/5833429/tom-wojcik)\n6,30944 gold badges3939 silver badges5555 bronze badges\n\n##\n                                        Your Answer\n\nThanks for contributing an answer to Stack Overflow!\n- Please be sure to answer the question. Provide details and share your research!\nBut avoid …\n- Asking for help, clarification, or responding to other answers.\n- Making statements based on opinion; back them up with references or personal experience.\nTo learn more, see our [tips on writing great answers](/help/how-to-answer).\n\n### Sign up or [log in](/users/login?ssrc=question_page&returnurl=https%3a%2f%2fstackoverflow.com%2fquestions%2f23351796%2fdjango-block-internet-connection-for-testing-purposes%23new-answer)\n\nSign up using Email and Password\n                        Submit\n\nBy clicking “Post Your Answer”, you agree to our [terms of service](https://stackoverflow.com/legal/terms-of-service/public) and acknowledge you have read our [privacy policy](https://stackoverflow.com/legal/privacy-policy).", "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "corpus_id": null}, {"id": "661f51_p9", "source_url": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/23351796/django-block-internet-connection-for-testing-purposes", "source_title": "python - Django: block internet connection for testing purposes - Stack Overflow", "text": "Start asking to get answers\nFind the answer to your question by asking.[Ask question](/questions/ask)\n\n- [python](/questions/tagged/python)\n- [django](/questions/tagged/django)\n- [unit-testing](/questions/tagged/unit-testing)\n- [testing](/questions/tagged/testing)\nSee similar questions with these tags.\n\n[Making the OWASP top ten in the vibe code...](https://stackoverflow.blog/2026/06/05/making-the-owasp-top-ten-in-the-vibe-code-era/)\n                            Featured on Meta\n\n[Native Ads Coming To Comments](https://meta.stackexchange.com/questions/418563/native-ads-coming-to-comments)\n\n[Policy: Generative AI (e.g., ChatGPT) is banned](https://meta.stackoverflow.com/questions/421831/policy-generative-ai-e-g-chatgpt-is-banned)\n\n[Help Shape the 2026 Developer Survey!](https://meta.stackoverflow.com/questions/438884/help-shape-the-2026-developer-survey)", "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "corpus_id": null}, {"id": "661f51_p10", "source_url": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/23351796/django-block-internet-connection-for-testing-purposes", "source_title": "python - Django: block internet connection for testing purposes - Stack Overflow", "text": "[\n52](/questions/18601828/python-block-network-connections-for-testing-purposes)[Python: block network connections for testing purposes?](/questions/18601828/python-block-network-connections-for-testing-purposes?noredirect=1)\n[\n4](/questions/3730286/how-can-i-disable-a-third-party-api-when-executing-django-unit-tests)[How can I disable a third party API when executing Django unit tests?](/questions/3730286/how-can-i-disable-a-third-party-api-when-executing-django-unit-tests?noredirect=1)", "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "corpus_id": null}, {"id": "661f51_p11", "source_url": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/23351796/django-block-internet-connection-for-testing-purposes", "source_title": "python - Django: block internet connection for testing purposes - Stack Overflow", "text": "[\n1](/questions/3030406/prevent-django-from-running-contrib-tests)[Prevent Django from running contrib tests?](/questions/3030406/prevent-django-from-running-contrib-tests)\n[\n3](/questions/7208096/django-testing-with-local-settings)[django testing with local settings](/questions/7208096/django-testing-with-local-settings)\n[\n4](/questions/8420710/how-to-tell-if-python-django-unit-tests-are-hitting-the-network)[How to tell if Python/Django unit tests are hitting the network?](/questions/8420710/how-to-tell-if-python-django-unit-tests-are-hitting-the-network)\n[\n2](/questions/14741515/how-to-disable-internal-django-tests)[How to disable internal django tests?](/questions/14741515/how-to-disable-internal-django-tests)\n[\n5](/questions/16507913/django-unittest-without-database-connection)[django unittest without database connection](/questions/16507913/django-unittest-without-database-connection)\n[\n2](/questions/28864152/django-tests-run-faster-with-no-internet-connection)[Django Tests run faster with no internet connection](/questions/28864152/django-tests-run-faster-with-no-internet-connection)\n[\n1](/questions/29023164/django-webserver-during-testing)[Django WebServer During Testing](/questions/29023164/django-webserver-during-testing)\n[\n3](/questions/36583653/django-tests-that-should-not-run-automatically)[Django tests that should not run automatically](/questions/36583653/django-tests-that-should-not-run-automatically)\n[\n1](/questions/44550805/how-to-disable-djangos-test-discovery)[How to disable Django's Test Discovery?](/questions/44550805/how-to-disable-djangos-test-discovery)\n[\n2](/questions/54432039/temporarily-disable-django-cache-during-unit-test)[Temporarily disable Django cache during Unit Test](/questions/54432039/temporarily-disable-django-cache-during-unit-test)", "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "corpus_id": null}], "fetched_ok": true, "error": null, "status": "ok"}}
harness/cassettes/research_demo.jsonl:21:{"seam": "extract", "key": "5a597c99b4f986a2", "input": {"url": "https://punksecurity.co.uk/blog/using_vcrpy/"}, "output": {"url": "https://punksecurity.co.uk/blog/using_vcrpy/", "title": "Punk Security - Streamline Your Tests: Mocking APIs with VCR.py", "content": "#  Streamline Your Tests: Mocking APIs with VCR.py\n\n#####\n\n##\n\t\t\t\t\t\tDevelopers typically use the unittest.mock module to patch the requests library directly, replacing real HTTP calls with mock objects.  vcrpy makes it much easier.\n\n# Streamline Your Tests: Mocking APIs with VCR.py\n\nTesting applications that interact with external services can be challenging. Third-party APIs are unpredictable; they can change, fail, or rate-limit your requests. That’s where mocking comes in handy. Traditionally, we’ve patched HTTP calls directly within our test suite, but vcrpy provides a more robust and cleaner approach because it automates some of the hard work for us.\n\n## Why do we need to test?\n\nTesting applications is important because it’s like a safety net when you’re adding new stuff or fixing things in your application, be that a conventional application or web.  Testing makes sure everything works like it’s supposed to and doesn’t mess up something else that was already fine. It’s kind of like a quality check that gives everyone involved, from the folks who build the app to the ones who use it, a big thumbs up that everything’s running smoothly. In a nutshell, good testing keeps the app reliable, and everyone using it happy and stress-free.\n\n### The Traditional Way: Patching requests\n\nDevelopers typically use the unittest.mock module to patch the requests library directly, replacing real HTTP calls with mock objects. Here’s how you might typically do this:\nimport requests\nfrom unittest.mock import patch, Mock\ndef get_user_data():\n    response = requests.get(\"https://punksecurity.co.uk/api/punks\")\nreturn response.json()\n@patch('requests.get')\ndef test_get_user_data(mock_get):\n    mock_get.return_value = Mock(ok=True)\n    mock_get.return_value.json.return_value = {\"name\": \"Simon Gurney\", \"id\": \"1337\"}\n    result = get_user_data()\nassert result == {\"name\": \"Simon Gurney\", \"id\": \"1337\"}\n\nThis approach works fine but can become unwieldy as the complexity of the API interactions grows.  You also need to provide that API response, or at least enough of it to make the test work.  For some APIs, this can become a chore in itself.\n\n### Enter VCR.py\n\nvcrpy simplifies this process by recording HTTP interactions and replaying them for your tests. It’s like a VCR for your API calls, hence the name. You make a real request once, vcrpy records it, and then during subsequent test runs, it plays back the recorded response.\n\n### Setting up vcrpy\n\nFirst, you’ll need to install vcrpy:\npip install vcrpy\n\n### Mocking with vcrpy\n\nHere’s how to mock an API call to get user data using vcrpy:\nimport requests\nimport vcr\ndef get_user_data():\n    response = requests.get(\"https://punksecurity.co.uk/api/punks\")\nreturn response.json()\n@vcr.use_cassette('fixtures/vcr_cassettes/punks.yaml')\ndef test_get_user_data_vcr():\n    result = get_user_data()\nassert result == {\"name\": \"Simon Gurney\", \"id\": \"1337\"}\n\nIn this code, @vcr.use_cassette wraps the test function. It records the HTTP request and response to a ‘cassette’ file on the first test run. On subsequent runs, vcrpy plays back the response from this file.\n\n### Advantages of using vcrpy\n\n- Maintainability: To update your test fixtures, you simply delete  the stored cassettes.\n- Reliability: Tests are not prone to failure due to API rate-limiting or downtime.\n- Simplicity: Tests are easier to read and maintain.\n- Quicker: You don’t need to manually capture api responses\n\n### Conclusion\n\nMocking API calls is an essential part of testing applications that rely on external services. While patching the requests library is a common approach, vcrpy offers a more advanced and streamlined method. By recording real HTTP interactions and replaying them, vcrpy makes your tests faster, more reliable, and a breeze to maintain. Give vcrpy a try, and you might find your tests are more like a walk in the park than a race through the wild web.\n\n### Need assistance?\n\nIf you need help building or improving your application unit or functional testing, we can help!\n\n######\n\t\t\t\t\t\tFor more information, email us at [[email protected]](/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection#1a73747c755a6a6f7471697f796f68736e63347975346f71) or call us on 01609 635 932\n\n##  Author\n\n#####\n\n## Simon Gurney MSc\n\n#### - CTO -\n\nSimon is one of the Punk Security Directors and has over 17 years experience working within IT, primarily focused on automation and InfoSec.[ read more ](https://punksecurity.co.uk/team/simon/)", "passages": [{"id": "bb5034_p0", "source_url": "https://punksecurity.co.uk/blog/using_vcrpy/", "source_title": "Punk Security - Streamline Your Tests: Mocking APIs with VCR.py", "text": "#  Streamline Your Tests: Mocking APIs with VCR.py\n\n##\n\t\t\t\t\t\tDevelopers typically use the unittest.mock module to patch the requests library directly, replacing real HTTP calls with mock objects.  vcrpy makes it much easier.\n\n# Streamline Your Tests: Mocking APIs with VCR.py\n\nTesting applications that interact with external services can be challenging. Third-party APIs are unpredictable; they can change, fail, or rate-limit your requests. That’s where mocking comes in handy. Traditionally, we’ve patched HTTP calls directly within our test suite, but vcrpy provides a more robust and cleaner approach because it automates some of the hard work for us.", "char_start": null, "char_end": null, "corpus_id": null}, {"id": "bb5034_p1", "source_url": "https://punksecurity.co.uk/blog/using_vcrpy/", "source_title": "Punk Security - Streamline Your Tests: Mocking APIs with VCR.py", "text": "Testing applications is 